      "source": [
        "@tbl-oos-performance reports the out-of-sample results. The Ledoit-Wolf strategy attains the\n",
        "lowest annualized volatility at `{python} f\"{lw_vol:.2f}\"`\\% with a Sharpe ratio of\n",
        "`{python} f\"{lw_sharpe:.3f}\"`, whereas the high-frequency strategy is both more volatile, at\n",
        "`{python} f\"{hf_vol:.2f}\"`\\%, and lower-Sharpe, at `{python} f\"{hf_sharpe:.3f}\"`. The\n",
        "equal-weighted benchmark has the highest volatility, `{python} f\"{ew_vol:.2f}\"`\\%, but also\n",
        "the highest Sharpe ratio at `{python} f\"{ew_sharpe:.3f}\"`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The high-frequency estimator therefore does not deliver a measurable advantage over Ledoit-Wolf\n",
        "shrinkage [@ledoit.2003; @ledoit.2004]. On the contrary, it is dominated on both dimensions: it is\n",
        "more volatile and has a lower Sharpe ratio than the shrinkage estimator, even before trading\n",
        "costs are taken into account. This is notable given that both are minimum-variance portfolios:\n",
        "Ledoit-Wolf achieves the lowest realized volatility of all three strategies, exactly as the\n",
        "minimum-variance objective intends, while the high-frequency estimator does not. The difference\n",
        "is the same instability documented in Exercise 4, where the high-frequency strategy rebalanced\n",
        "with an average turnover of roughly 349\\% against only 7\\% for Ledoit-Wolf. Once realistic\n",
        "transaction costs are accounted for, the small amount of risk the high-frequency estimator does\n",
        "remove would be far outweighed by the cost of its trading, so the richer intraday information it\n",
        "exploits is not translated into an economically meaningful improvement. As seen in\n",
        "@fig-oos-cumulative-returns, the high-frequency strategy also displays the most violent swings\n",
        "in cumulative return, including a sharp drawdown in 2025, while Ledoit-Wolf is the smoothest of\n",
        "the three.\n",
        "\n",
        "The broader result is that neither optimized strategy beats the naive equal-weighted benchmark\n",
        "on a risk-adjusted basis: the benchmark, which estimates nothing and therefore carries no\n",
        "estimation error, attains the highest Sharpe ratio of the three. Whether an estimator that\n",
        "exploits additional information — intraday returns, shrinkage targets, or factor structures —\n",
        "improves on equal weighting depends on whether its reduction in estimation error outweighs the\n",
        "noise it introduces through the inversion of the covariance matrix. As emphasised in the\n",
        "in-class discussion and in the shrinkage literature [@ledoit2004], the sample covariance matrix\n",
        "is poorly conditioned when the number of assets is large relative to the sample, and imposing\n",
        "structure can substantially improve out-of-sample behaviour; this is precisely why Ledoit-Wolf\n",
        "outperforms the high-frequency estimator here. We would expect sophistication to pay off when\n",
        "the sample is long relative to the number of assets, when the covariance structure is stable and\n",
        "persistent so that past estimates forecast future risk well, and when turnover is controlled or\n",
        "trading costs are low. The minimum-variance problem with thirty assets and a covariance matrix\n",
        "re-estimated daily sits at the opposite end of this spectrum: estimation error dominates,\n",
        "structure (Ledoit-Wolf) helps more than extra information (high-frequency data), and even then\n",
        "the simplest possible rule is hard to beat. Our evidence suggests that statistical sophistication\n",
        "is valuable in portfolio choice mainly insofar as it stabilises the inputs to optimisation, and\n",
        "that sophistication which merely increases responsiveness — as with the daily-updated\n",
        "high-frequency estimator — does not reliably beat a simple benchmark once realistic frictions are\n",
        "considered."
